  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM EDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe large scale pattern can be classified by ridging generally \ncentered over the Four Corners region and Intermountain West the \nnext couple of days, followed by weak NWly flow over us with \ntroughing to the north and subsequent shortwaves cycling through. A \nTropical Depression by the Florida Peninsula will become almost \nstationary until it gets picked up and eventually drifts westward. A \nstout shortwave expected to dive SE across the northern Great Lakes \nbefore curving up around the U.S. border late tomorrow into \nWednesday, will bring our next frontal system across the area. \nFollowing that, we'll still be situated east of upper ridge into the \nlatter part of the week and into the weekend.\n\nDiurnal thunderstorm activity continues today much like the past \ncouple of days, except the mountainous terrain will likely see the \nbest chance during the afternoon and evening. The west to east \nboundary that was draped near the TN and VA border yesterday, will \nbe situated a bit further north today. A few strong strong to severe \nstorms are possible due to moderate cape, but shear will continue to \nbe weak. The main threat with any stronger storm will be localized \ngusty winds. Heavy rain is also expected as PWATS are around 2 \ninches and this may lead to isolated flooding for some. Any area \nthat sees repeat heavy showers through this evening will be most at \nrisk for flooding. Again, gusty wind threat and flood threat will be \nisolated. The stronger storm and flooding threat will likely be for \nareas east of I-75.\n\nAttention turns to tomorrow into Wednesday with a potential strong \ncold frontal passage. SPC introduced a slight risk for severe \nweather for the northern part of the CWA as of this discussion. The \nmain threats are damaging winds and heavy rain that may lead to \nflash flooding. Secondary threats that cannot be ruled out are a \ncouple of tornados and possible small hail. The tornado threat will \nultimately depend on whether storms remain surface-based during the \novernight hours or become elevated. Timing will definitely play a \nrole as to when the main line arrives and what it means for our \narea, so there's still some uncertainty. One factor that models have \nconsistently shown the past couple of nights is increased shear.\n\nFollowing the mid-week cold front, Thursday will be very nice with \nlow to mid 80s highs and dew points in the 60s under high pressure. \nTemperatures rise a couple of degrees into the weekend, but for the \nmost part remain below normal. A shortwave cycling the top of the \nupper ridge Friday into Saturday, may bring precipitation chances \nback to the area and unfortunately increased dew points yet again. A \npossible boundary that could get hung up to our south may complicate \nthings.",
